#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069690} :activity/id #uuid "8f764106-b0e7-5f8e-bf19-c530d505500a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048643 :topic/id #uuid "b16c2962-e115-56a0-9029-0d281a36443f" :topic/math-academy-id 414} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069920 :tutorial/content "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x,$ and the vertical line $x=2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/65/651d5d0d9ccafdeb94608e74086136b121c394670afbea669c3e99606f9756aa.png)\n\nWe rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis to get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/7a/7a1e4b8d0a27283cad7a0b0e0f2b23f1a45d0e560582a61f84c0f90a4f61e039.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the outer curve $y=f(x),$ the inner curve $y=g(x),$ and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\Big( \\big[ \\underbrace{f(x)}_\\text{outer} \\big]^2 - \\big[ \\underbrace{g(x)}_\\text{inner} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method of computing the volume is called the **washer method** because it involves breaking up the region into many thin washers (one such washer is shown below). \n\n![](images/29/2920f308f15ef81eaff0ba431d0f4eec3ccd9826fbde8f126242b0493f1f8e32.png)\n\nThe cross-sectional area of each washer corresponds to the region between two discs of radius $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ so the area of each washer cross-section is \n\n$$\n\\pi [f(x)]^2 - \\pi [g(x)]^2,\n$$\n\nand this is what we integrate.\n\nIn our case, the outer curve is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and the inner curve is $g(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x,$ so the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{2}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi ((\\frac{4}{2}-0)-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{8}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the volume of the solid is $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ cubic units." :tutorial/id #uuid "26f7c4eb-b5c8-505b-9621-32b5c0241376" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1058 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b04f3bdc-ba1f-5240-97c4-3e1c70fbfd66" :step/math-academy-id 639680 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068617 :question/id #uuid "3a0a00c8-8a86-52de-bc9d-d9219a0735cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-591"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e9590de-9413-5bec-b3f4-315038a3848e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529156}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "436583be-c75f-59e7-86c9-372a731d2148" :step/math-academy-id 639684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069921 :tutorial/content "Consider the plane region bounded by the curve ![](images/da/da9e7c622c9bb4816126baaf7030000b0df743977b620a20454f6f41974d4a66.svg) the curve ![](images/34/344c03d67f3618b1dc0560c072fc86443bf8eb991de0eed6ed1196fe20586053.svg) and the horizontal line ![](images/51/5105b7bf189fe285a6ab74d0f01cdea25d5acb40cac53551b0ac58def8d4180f.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c3d